(488971, 45665, 8860, '<td valign="top">This is definitely number one for me. Sumptuous tones and subtle lighting, great angle, beautiful texture. Great title too - a master at work for sure.<br/> <br/>Shame on that person who voted 2 (or any below 7 to be frank).</td>', 'This is definitely number one for me. Sumptuous tones and subtle lighting, great angle, beautiful texture. Great title too - a master at work for sure. Shame on that person who voted 2 (or any below 7 to be frank).', '2003-11-18 15:36:04.000000', NULL, 0),
(488992, 45999, 9411, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club,</b> <br/>I was just reviewing what I mentioned during the challenge in my commnet to you.<br/> <br/>Looking at this image again, I would say possibley a little lower angle to capture the light rays, but keep it out of the viewers eyes, which is how it feels now.<br/> <br/>Can you set the iso on the cd400? If so try going down to 50 to remove some of the grain.<br/> <br/>Backlit shots can be very tricky and if you have a camera that does not allow manual adjustments it''s going to be a challenge for sure.<br/> <br/>You may want to try using a polarizing filter of some sort also. Even if you can''t actually attach it to the camera you can hold it in front of the lense, polarized sunglasses work too.<br/> <br/>As I said in my previouse comments, this could be a really good image.<br/> <br/>I hope this helps.<br/> <br/>JC<br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>I would also try different Brightness and Contrast settings if you have the software to do this with.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club, I was just reviewing what I mentioned during the challenge in my commnet to you. Looking at this image again, I would say possibley a little lower angle to capture the light rays, but keep it out of the viewers eyes, which is how it feels now. Can you set the iso on the cd400? If so try going down to 50 to remove some of the grain. Backlit shots can be very tricky and if you have a camera that does not allow manual adjustments it''s going to be a challenge for sure. You may want to try using a polarizing filter of some sort also. Even if you can''t actually attach it to the camera you can hold it in front of the lense, polarized sunglasses work too. As I said in my previouse comments, this could be a really good image. I hope this helps. JC   I would also try different Brightness and Contrast settings if you have the software to do this with.', '2003-11-18 15:47:39.000000', NULL, 0),
